after the rains the cleanup and a  rethink on how to adapt to a changing  climate  the worst affected places were in  low-lying areas south of seoul's han  river  it's home to the glitzy gangnam district  made famous in the hit gangnam style  [Music]  but it's also the location for poorer  housing including half basement  apartments prone to flooding resident  hain sick tries to salvage what he can  from his home  when the rains came he and his family  were forced to evacuate seeking shelter  in a nearby park  i have to throw away all my possessions  even my dishes it rained so much this  time it was a disaster  the plight of such residents was  highlighted in the oscar-winning movie  parasite about a fictional family living  a subterranean existence  but it's the reality for many in a city  with sky-high property prices  it's different from the wealthy places  we live in the smaller streets where the  government doesn't pay attention to us  south korea's president yun sukyol  visited one basement apartment that was  flooded and where three people died the  response from seoul city has been swift  with the government announcing no new  permissions will be given to turn  basements into apartments but there are  reckoned to be around two hundred  thousand homes below ground right now  that authorities say will gradually be  phased out the government has also  announced plans for better drainage  adapting it says to the new reality that  such extreme weather is now no longer  abnormal  rob mcbride al jazeera seoul
 

